1
00:00:01,386 --> 00:00:26,228
This is JuriSense Gate. It turns contracts, supplier data, and company policies into
auditable compliance decisions. Acme Tecnologia, a two hundred person Brazilian SaaS
company, needs to hire a new vendor. This organization and its compliance policy were
configured ahead of time. What you are about to see is the live supplier and contract
workflow.

2
00:00:28,228 --> 00:00:37,853
First, the supplier. I create CloudDocs Software as a new supplier, using its tax
I.D. It starts in prospect status.

3
00:00:38,559 --> 00:00:56,672
Submitting the tax I.D. triggers a supplier check, a point in time verification
against official sources: company registry status, and the sanctions lists. This
check is immutable once it completes. We never overwrite a verification run, we
create a new one.

4
00:00:58,858 --> 00:01:13,124
Now the contract. I upload Cloud Docs' proposed SaaS agreement. On upload, the system
computes a SHA two fifty six hash of the document. This is what eventually chains
into the Evidence Package.

5
00:01:13,826 --> 00:01:17,487
A.I. extraction is running against the contract now.

6
00:01:26,389 --> 00:01:51,764
And here are the extracted terms. Contract value, four hundred eighty thousand reais
a year. Term, twenty four months. Unlimited liability. It processes personal data,
yes. A Data Processing Agreement, absent. Every one of these fields traces back to a
specific clause in the source document. This is provenance, not a black box summary.

7
00:01:52,738 --> 00:01:57,082
Now I trigger a compliance review against Acme's published policy.

8
00:01:57,786 --> 00:02:28,936
Three rules fired. L.G.P.D. zero zero one: the contract processes personal data but
has no D.P.A., that's a BLOCK, critical severity. LEGAL zero zero eight: unlimited
liability, REVIEW. FIN zero zero three: contract value over the threshold, REVIEW.
Here's the product's core explainability claim: even though two rules only said
REVIEW, one rule said BLOCK, and BLOCK always wins, because it's the safer failure
mode.

9
00:02:31,061 --> 00:02:43,678
CloudDocs corrects the contract. They add a D.P.A. and cap liability. This is a brand
new contract version, never an edit to the old one, which stays exactly as it was.

10
00:02:44,379 --> 00:02:47,927
A.I. extraction runs again on the corrected version.

11
00:03:05,152 --> 00:03:09,368
A new compliance review runs against the same published policy.

12
00:03:10,069 --> 00:03:39,651
L.G.P.D. zero zero one now passes. Liability is no longer unlimited. But FIN zero
zero three still fires, the contract value hasn't changed. Aggregation now yields
REVIEW, not APPROVE. This is realistic, not a scripted everything turns green moment.
A R. four hundred eighty thousand, twenty four month contract still needs a human
legal sign off on value alone, even after the compliance blocker is fixed.

13
00:03:47,091 --> 00:04:02,499
Because this is REVIEW, an approval request routes to whoever holds the Legal role in
this organization, not a hardcoded person. Logged in as Acme's Legal approver, I see
the full rationale, and I approve it, with a justification note.

14
00:04:04,299 --> 00:04:31,218
With the decision resolved, the system packages a Decision Passport: the decision,
the supplier, the contract, document hashes, the exact policy version evaluated,
every rule evaluation, including the historical BLOCK, the A.I.'s own execution
provenance, the approver, and the timestamp. Ask why was this approved, two years
from now, and this is the complete, reproducible answer.

15
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:48,721
A note here: the blockchain anchor for this decision was pre submitted a few minutes
before this recording. Constellation's network needs time to reach consensus, so we
don't sit here waiting on camera. Only the next step, verify, is live.

16
00:04:50,175 --> 00:04:59,695
Last step, the public verification page. No login required. That's the whole point of
a public, third party checkable proof.

17
00:05:04,261 --> 00:05:23,393
I click Verify, live, right now. And it confirms the hash matches what was anchored.
Anyone, an auditor, a regulator, an acquirer's due diligence team, can independently
confirm this decision's evidence hasn't been tampered with, without trusting
JuriSense's word for it.

18
00:05:24,095 --> 00:05:32,905
That's JuriSense Gate. Contracts, supplier data, and company policies, turned into
auditable compliance decisions.
